const faqs = [
  { q: "What makes a good construction photo?", a: "People, scale, and clean light. A person in the frame gives the work size and proves a crew did it, and PPE worn correctly is what makes the photo publishable. Shoot in morning or late afternoon light, clear the trash and loose gear out of the frame first, and get close enough that the subject fills the shot. A photo of a specific moment, a pick, a pour, a panel being set, will always beat a wide shot of a site where nothing is happening." },
  { q: "Can we just use phone photos?", a: "Yes, for most of what marketing actually needs. A current phone in good light, held level and framed clean, is plenty for your website, social posts, proposals, and internal recruiting. It falls short for large-format printing, trade-show graphics, dim plant interiors, and any image that has to hold up at full-bleed on a homepage. Use the phone for the running record of every project, and bring in a professional for the handful of images that have to carry real weight." },
  { q: "When should we photograph a project?", a: "During construction at the milestones: mobilization, first pick, topping out, enclosure. Then again after occupancy, once the fence is gone, the landscaping is in, and people are using the building. The finished shot sells hardest, because owners and developers are buying a finished building, not a jobsite. The milestone shots prove you did the work, and the finished shot proves the work was worth buying." },
  { q: "Who owns the photos a photographer takes?", a: "In general the photographer holds the copyright unless your agreement says otherwise, so settle usage before the shoot, not after. Ask for written rights covering the uses you actually have: website, proposals, social, trade-show graphics, recruiting. Some photographers license images by use and others will transfer broad rights for a higher fee, and both arrangements are normal. Whatever you agree to, get it in writing before anyone raises a camera." },
];
---

