  <Prose tag="The case" heading="Why does construction photography usually fail?">
    <p>Because the photos get taken for documentation, not for selling. The camera comes out when something needs to be recorded: a punch list item, a damaged panel, rebar spacing before a cover-up, a trench before backfill. Those photos do their job, and then they are worthless for anything else. A year later, when someone asks for project photos for the website or a proposal, the folder holds four hundred images of mud, conduit, and the backs of hard hats, all shot at noon under a gray sky.</p>
    <p>This is a strange failure, because construction sits on the best raw material in business. A crane pick against a morning sky. A crew setting a forty-foot panel. Steel, concrete, height, weather, and scale. Software companies pay agencies to fake this kind of drama with stock imagery and staged laptops. You have the real thing on site every week, and the record of it dies in a documentation folder nobody will ever open for marketing.</p>
    <p>A usable photo was planned before it was taken. Someone knew the pick was happening at nine, knew where the sun would be, walked the loose gear and trash out of the frame, and stood in the right spot with a clean lens. None of that requires equipment. A phone in the right hands at the right moment beats an expensive camera at the wrong one, and a professional photographer at substantial completion beats both.</p>
    <p>So the fix is a habit, not a purchase. Keep a standing shot list that never changes from project to project. Tie it to milestones so nobody has to remember to shoot, because the schedule remembers for them. Assign it to one person by name on every job, since a photo that is everyone's responsibility is no one's. The list below is the one to start with.</p>
  </Prose>

  <Checklist
