  <Prose tag="When to hire it" heading="When a professional is worth it" mist={true} marks={true}>
    <p>Hire one for the projects that will carry your marketing for years. Substantial completion of signature work is the clearest case. The finished building, the finished parking structure, the finished plant expansion: these are the images that end up on the homepage, on the proposal cover, and on the trade-show wall, and they have to stay sharp at large sizes in light a phone cannot flatter. An aerial day is the second case, because an FAA-certificated drone pilot working under Part 107 gets angles nothing on the ground can, and the airspace authorizations are their problem instead of yours. A recruiting shoot is the third, because photos of your real people doing real work outperform anything staged, and a good photographer gets them without stopping production.</p>
    <p>What you hand the photographer matters as much as who you hire. Give them the shot list from this page, plus whatever is specific to the project. Tell them exactly where the images will live: website, proposals, trade-show graphics, recruiting pages. A photographer who knows the end use frames for it, leaving room for a headline on one shot and shooting vertical for another. And give them access at the right phase. A building photographs best when the finishes are in, before the fence comes down and the lot fills with other people's trucks. Book the photographer when you schedule the milestone, not after it has passed and the moment is gone.</p>
    <p>One note on scope. A single professional day at completion, layered on a season of milestone phone photos from the standing list, covers nearly every marketing need a producer or contractor has. You do not need a photographer on retainer. You need one good day, on the right day, with clear instructions in hand.</p>
  </Prose>
